## PHASE 3 — Deploy your backend

Your GitHub repo has **both** `backend` and `frontend` at the root, so you clone once at `/home`, then `cd` two levels into `backend`. It is **not** `cd backend` right after login — the repo folder has to exist first.

### Step 11. Clone your repository
```
cd /home
git clone <YOUR_GITHUB_REPOSITORY_URL>
```

Example:
```
git clone https://github.com/<YOUR_USERNAME>/<YOUR_REPOSITORY>.git
```

This creates:
```
/home/<YOUR_REPOSITORY>/
├── backend/
└── frontend/
```

### Step 12. Enter the backend folder
```
cd <YOUR_REPOSITORY>/backend
```
(e.g. `cd /home/lovecruz/backend` if your repo is named `lovecruz`)

### Step 13. Install backend dependencies
```
npm install
```

### Step 14. Create your PostgreSQL database and user (local Postgres, on the VPS)

This is the step that changes once you move from a managed/cloud Postgres (like whatever you used on Render) to Postgres running **on the VPS itself**. Instead of pointing `DATABASE_URL` at an external host, you point it at `localhost`, because the database now lives on the same machine as your app.

Switch to the `postgres` system user and open the Postgres shell:
```
sudo -u postgres psql
```

Inside the `psql` prompt, create a database and a dedicated app user (replace the password):
```sql
CREATE DATABASE lovecruz;
CREATE USER lovecruz_user WITH ENCRYPTED PASSWORD '<YOUR_DB_PASSWORD>';
GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON DATABASE lovecruz TO lovecruz_user;
```

Exit:
```sql
\q
```

Your new local connection string will look like:
```
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://lovecruz_user:<YOUR_DB_PASSWORD>@localhost:5432/lovecruz
```

**Important — SSL:** your `db.js` sets:
```js
ssl: {
  rejectUnauthorized: false,
}
```
That's meant for cloud Postgres providers (Render, Supabase, RDS, etc.) that require SSL. A fresh local `postgresql` install on the VPS does **not** have SSL enabled by default, and connecting with an `ssl` object against a non-SSL local server can throw `The server does not support SSL connections`. Update `db.js` so SSL is only applied for non-local connections:

```js
const pool = new Pool({
  connectionString: process.env.DATABASE_URL,
  ssl: process.env.DATABASE_URL.includes("localhost")
    ? false
    : { rejectUnauthorized: false },
  max: 20,
  idleTimeoutMillis: 30000,
  connectionTimeoutMillis: 10000,
  allowExitOnIdle: false,
});
```

No changes are needed to `pg_hba.conf` or `postgresql.conf` for this — since your app and Postgres run on the same VPS, `localhost` connections are allowed by default. You'd only touch those files if something *else* (a different server, your own laptop, etc.) needed to connect to this Postgres instance remotely.

### Step 15. Create your `.env` file
```
nano .env
```

Paste your environment variables, replacing placeholders:
```
MEDIASOUP_ANNOUNCED_IP=<YOUR_VPS_PUBLIC_IP>
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://lovecruz_user:<YOUR_DB_PASSWORD>@localhost:5432/lovecruz
REDIS_URL=<YOUR_REDIS_URL>
TURN_PASSWORD=<YOUR_TURN_PASSWORD>
```

Save and exit nano:
```
Ctrl + O   (write)
Enter
Ctrl + X   (exit)
```

### Step 16. Run database migrations
```
npm run migrate
```
or, depending on your project structure:
```
node scripts/migrate.js
```

### Step 17. Start the backend with PM2
```
pm2 start server.js --name lovecruz-api
```
or:
```
pm2 start src/server.js --name lovecruz-api
```
(use whichever entry file matches your project)

### Step 18. Persist PM2 across reboots
```
pm2 save
pm2 startup
```
`pm2 startup` will print a second command — copy that exact line, paste it, and run it. Then run:
```
pm2 save
```
again.

### Step 19. Verify the backend is running
```
pm2 status
```
or
```
pm2 logs
```
You should see `lovecruz-api` with status `online`, and the startup log confirming `DATABASE_URL: Loaded ✅`.

### (Optional) Step 19b. Build and serve the frontend on the same VPS
```
cd ../frontend
npm install
npm run build
```
The build output (usually `dist/`) can then be served by Nginx as static files — happy to add that Nginx block if you want the frontend hosted here too, rather than on Vercel/Netlify.

